it seems clear that today faith is  thriving in many parts of the world it  seems also clear that faith is not just  thriving but at various faiths are  increasingly finding it important to  insert themselves into public realm it  is equally clear that the idea of  democracy is popular in the world today  just about all parts of the globe and  then the question becomes what is the  relationship between faith and faith  which refuses to be simply private and  the idea and the practice of democracy  this question is particularly  significant as we live in an  increasingly pluralistic world so that  various policies themselves are  increasingly internally pluralistic  various worldviews vie for attention and  express interests and needs and then the  question becomes how does one negotiate  between the claims of one faiths to be  the faith to be heard and the democratic  process this is the topic for our  discussion today under what condition if  at all is it permitted for faith to  express itself and be brought to bear in  the public realm what ways can be  designed so that that faiths insertion  into the public realm is part and parcel  of regular democratic process this is a  significant issue both because of the  vibrancy of faith in the world today and  because of the attractiveness of the  idea of democracy
 

